1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,651
<font color="#000000">This paper, A View From Somewhere,</font>

2
00:00:02,731 --> 00:00:07,703
<font color="#000000">published at ICLR 2023 by researchers at Sony AI
and the University of Tokyo,</font>

3
00:00:07,783 --> 00:00:10,984
<font color="#000000">rethinks how we represent human faces in datasets.</font>

4
00:00:11,064 --> 00:00:15,774
<font color="#000000">Instead of relying on problematic demographic
labels like race or gender,</font>

5
00:00:15,854 --> 00:00:21,679
<font color="#000000">the authors collect over six hundred thousand human
judgments of face similarity and learn</font>

6
00:00:21,759 --> 00:00:25,550
<font color="#000000">a continuous embedding space aligned with human
perception.</font>

7
00:00:25,630 --> 00:00:29,356
<font color="#000000">The result is a face representation that is interpretable,</font>

8
00:00:29,436 --> 00:00:33,949
<font color="#000000">that avoids invasive categorical labels, and that
captures the smooth,</font>

9
00:00:34,029 --> 00:00:37,098
<font color="#000000">continuous nature of human phenotypic diversity,</font>

10
00:00:37,178 --> 00:00:41,560
<font color="#000000">opening a new path for measuring dataset diversity
more responsibly.</font>

11
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:46,313
<font color="#000000">Evaluating the diversity of face datasets almost
always relies on categorical demographic labels such as race,</font>

12
00:00:46,393 --> 00:00:50,767
<font color="#000000">gender, or age.</font>

13
00:00:50,847 --> 00:00:55,220
<font color="#000000">But these labels are frequently unavailable,
legally restricted to collect,</font>

14
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:59,673
<font color="#000000">and prone to bias when they are inferred by
annotators rather than self-reported.</font>

15
00:00:59,753 --> 00:01:04,127
<font color="#000000">Worse,</font>

16
00:01:04,207 --> 00:01:08,580
<font color="#000000">categorical labels flatten the continuous nature of
human appearance: skin tone becomes simply light or dark,</font>

17
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:13,033
<font color="#000000">and two faces sharing the same label can still look
strikingly different.</font>

18
00:01:13,113 --> 00:01:17,487
<font color="#000000">Rigid taxonomies also erase multi-ethnic people,
shift meaning across cultures,</font>

19
00:01:17,567 --> 00:01:21,940
<font color="#000000">and can cause real psychological harm when someone
is mislabeled.</font>

20
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:28,770
<font color="#000000">The authors argue that face similarity is
fundamentally continuous and depends on who is doing the judging.</font>

21
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:33,286
<font color="#000000">If we could learn a representation directly aligned
with human perception,</font>

22
00:01:33,366 --> 00:01:38,596
<font color="#000000">we could sidestep problematic semantic labels
entirely while still measuring diversity.</font>

23
00:01:38,676 --> 00:01:45,797
<font color="#000000">Existing psychological-embedding methods fall
short: they either cannot embed new faces beyond the fixed training set,</font>

24
00:01:45,877 --> 00:01:48,056
<font color="#000000">or they pool every judgment together,</font>

25
00:01:48,136 --> 00:01:53,060
<font color="#000000">throwing away the meaningful differences within and
between individual annotators.</font>

26
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:57,322
<font color="#000000">Their answer is A View From Somewhere, or AVFS.</font>

27
00:01:57,403 --> 00:02:01,285
<font color="#000000">They collect a dataset of six hundred thirty-eight
thousand triplet similarity judgments</font>

28
00:02:01,365 --> 00:02:05,248
<font color="#000000">over nearly five thousand faces,</font>

29
00:02:05,328 --> 00:02:09,210
<font color="#000000">and crucially,</font>

30
00:02:09,290 --> 00:02:13,173
<font color="#000000">each judgment records the identity and
self-reported demographics of the annotator who made it.</font>

31
00:02:13,253 --> 00:02:17,135
<font color="#000000">On top of this data they build a conditional
learning framework that produces a continuous,</font>

32
00:02:17,215 --> 00:02:21,098
<font color="#000000">low-dimensional, and human-interpretable embedding space,</font>

33
00:02:21,178 --> 00:02:25,060
<font color="#000000">along with per-annotator masks that reveal how
different people weigh different visual dimensions.</font>

34
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:29,754
<font color="#000000">At the core is a conditional convolutional network,
a ResNet eighteen,</font>

35
00:02:29,834 --> 00:02:34,274
<font color="#000000">that maps each face to a
one-hundred-twenty-eight-dimensional embedding.</font>

36
00:02:34,354 --> 00:02:38,166
<font color="#000000">Training uses odd-one-out judgments: given a
triplet of faces,</font>

37
00:02:38,246 --> 00:02:40,739
<font color="#000000">an annotator picks the least similar one.</font>

38
00:02:40,819 --> 00:02:45,573
<font color="#000000">Each annotator is assigned a learnable gating mask,
passed through a sigmoid,</font>

39
00:02:45,653 --> 00:02:50,092
<font color="#000000">that scales the importance of every embedding
dimension for that person.</font>

40
00:02:50,172 --> 00:02:54,110
<font color="#000000">Similarity between two faces is the dot product of
their masked,</font>

41
00:02:54,190 --> 00:02:55,428
<font color="#000000">rectified embeddings,</font>

42
00:02:55,508 --> 00:03:01,642
<font color="#000000">and the model predicts the odd-one-out probability
directly from these three pairwise similarities.</font>

43
00:03:01,722 --> 00:03:06,727
<font color="#000000">A sparsity and non-negativity penalty keeps the
dimensions few and interpretable,</font>

44
00:03:06,807 --> 00:03:09,740
<font color="#000000">leaving only about twenty-two active dimensions.</font>

45
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:15,658
<font color="#000000">The AVFS dataset comprises six hundred thirty-eight
thousand quality-controlled triplet</font>

46
00:03:15,738 --> 00:03:20,696
<font color="#000000">judgments over four thousand nine hundred
twenty-one near-frontal face images.</font>

47
00:03:20,776 --> 00:03:27,154
<font color="#000000">Every single judgment carries the annotator's
identifier and self-identified demographic attributes.</font>

48
00:03:27,234 --> 00:03:31,351
<font color="#000000">The authors evaluate on held-out judgments over the
same stimuli,</font>

49
00:03:31,431 --> 00:03:34,903
<font color="#000000">on eighty thousand judgments over entirely novel faces,</font>

50
00:03:34,983 --> 00:03:40,780
<font color="#000000">and against attribute-recognition baselines trained
on CelebA and FairFace semantic labels.</font>

51
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:45,343
<font color="#000000">Across both the same-stimuli and the novel-stimuli
evaluations,</font>

52
00:03:45,422 --> 00:03:49,605
<font color="#000000">the AVFS models predict human similarity judgments
more accurately than baselines trained on semantic labels,</font>

53
00:03:49,685 --> 00:03:53,867
<font color="#000000">and they better capture the uncertainty in human
judgments as measured by Spearman correlation.</font>

54
00:03:53,947 --> 00:03:58,130
<font color="#000000">Importantly,</font>

55
00:03:58,210 --> 00:04:02,392
<font color="#000000">the annotator-specific masks generalize even to
triplets made entirely of novel,</font>

56
00:04:02,472 --> 00:04:06,655
<font color="#000000">previously unseen faces,</font>

57
00:04:06,735 --> 00:04:10,917
<font color="#000000">which shows the embedding space is far more closely
aligned with the human mental representation</font>

58
00:04:10,997 --> 00:04:15,180
<font color="#000000">of faces than spaces learned from rigid categorical
labels.</font>

59
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:17,999
<font color="#000000">To test whether annotators really matter,</font>

60
00:04:18,079 --> 00:04:23,653
<font color="#000000">the authors randomly shuffle which annotator mask
is attached to each of the eighty thousand</font>

61
00:04:23,733 --> 00:04:26,971
<font color="#000000">judgments and recompute accuracy a hundred times over.</font>

62
00:04:27,051 --> 00:04:31,887
<font color="#000000">Accuracy drops from about sixty-two percent down to
roughly fifty-three percent,</font>

63
00:04:31,967 --> 00:04:35,451
<font color="#000000">proving that annotators are genuinely not interchangeable.</font>

64
00:04:35,531 --> 00:04:40,797
<font color="#000000">A dimension-elimination analysis further shows that
only six to thirteen dimensions are</font>

65
00:04:40,877 --> 00:04:43,870
<font color="#000000">needed to recover most of the predictive accuracy,</font>

66
00:04:43,950 --> 00:04:49,585
<font color="#000000">while fifteen to twenty-two dimensions are required
to explain the full similarity structure,</font>

67
00:04:49,665 --> 00:04:52,780
<font color="#000000">clear evidence that similarity is context-dependent.</font>

68
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:55,733
<font color="#000000">The headline numbers tell the whole story.</font>

69
00:04:55,813 --> 00:05:03,124
<font color="#000000">Six hundred thirty-eight thousand human judgments
gathered over four thousand nine hundred twenty-one distinct faces.</font>

70
00:05:03,204 --> 00:05:09,630
<font color="#000000">Twenty-two interpretable embedding dimensions
retained at nearly sixty-two percent validation accuracy.</font>

71
00:05:09,710 --> 00:05:12,410
<font color="#000000">And when annotator identities are scrambled,</font>

72
00:05:12,490 --> 00:05:17,463
<font color="#000000">accuracy falls from sixty-one point seven down to
fifty-two point eight percent,</font>

73
00:05:17,543 --> 00:05:21,380
<font color="#000000">all achieved without ever training on a single
semantic label.</font>

74
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:27,005
<font color="#000000">The key takeaway is that learning face
representations from human similarity judgments,</font>

75
00:05:27,085 --> 00:05:31,167
<font color="#000000">instead of demographic labels, produces a
continuous, interpretable,</font>

76
00:05:31,247 --> 00:05:37,900
<font color="#000000">and perception-aligned embedding space that
supports diversity analysis without invasive categorical labeling.</font>

77
00:05:37,980 --> 00:05:43,593
<font color="#000000">And because the framework only requires keeping
track of which annotator made which judgment,</font>

78
00:05:43,673 --> 00:05:48,857
<font color="#000000">the same conditional approach can extend naturally
to almost any machine-learning task</font>

79
00:05:48,937 --> 00:05:51,060
<font color="#000000">built on subjective human decisions.</font>
